==German==
{{wp|lang=de}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|de|Scheisse||Switzerland|Liechtenstein}}
* {{alt|de|Sch**ße|Sch**sse||censored}}
* {{alt|de|Scheiß}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|schīze}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*skītaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*skitiz}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*skeyd-||to part}}. 

Germanic cognates include {{cog|en|shit}}, {{cog|nds|Schiet}}, {{cog|nl|schijt}}, {{cog|fy|skyt}}, {{cog|sv|skit}}. Low German cognates sometimes used in standard German are {{m|de|Schiet}} and {{m|de|Schitte}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈʃaɪ̯sə/}}
* {{audio|de|De-Scheiße.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f.sg}}

# {{lb|de|vulgar}} [[feces]], [[shit]]
#: {{syn|de|Exkrement|Kacke|Kot|Stuhl|Scheiß}}
#: {{ux|de|Wo ich hintrete nur '''Scheiße'''!|Where I go, there is only '''shit'''!}}
# {{lb|de|vulgar|figuratively}} something worthless, [[shit]]
#: {{ux|de|Das ist doch alles '''Scheiße'''!|It's a load of '''crap'''! / It's a waste of time! / This is so fucked up! / This is '''bullshit'''!}}
# {{lb|de|vulgar}} [[nothing]], [[jack shit]], [[fuck all]]

====Usage notes====
While a direct cognate with [[shit]] in English, ''Scheiße'' can be used as a catch-all term for all forms of vulgarity and swear words in German.

